From: Christopher Owen <3221756+christopherowen@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 17:48:23 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 501/503] [DS4.1] DSpark draft windows start at CED's replay
 start

With a CED decoder, DSpark's context KV is written for the decoder's kept
rows only (CEDState's indices pack the drafter's context), and the
drafter's three caches are not prefix-cached. Its window metadata
(TileLang chunk / decode_metadata, Triton _chunk, DRAFT) started a window
before the anchor regardless, so two steps' draft rows read slots nothing
had written in the blocks' current ownership:

- a one-row nonfinal chunk (nonfinal_limits): only its last row has draft
  context, and the window read the 127 positions before it;
- a short first chunk after a prefix hit at L: the window read back into
  [L - 128, L), freshly allocated and never written.

Both chunks are nonfinal, so their drafts are dropped (update_draft_token_ids
ignores prefill chunks) and no output or acceptance changed. But the rows
are routed: zeroing made those records finite, and stale bytes (NaN as E4M3
0x7F/0xFF or a UE8M0 0xFF scale) would trip the router's non-finite
device_assert.

CED's replay start is exactly where a request's draft context begins (the
first kept row, or its admission prefix), so draft windows now start no
earlier, as the decoder's do. The model state binds its persistent
replay_start buffer to the drafter's attention layers, so captured draft
graphs read each step's values; the drafter declares only the replay
variant. Decode and final chunks are unchanged: their windows already lie
inside written rows (the final chunk keeps at least 128).

Tests: the TileLang window metadata equals the Triton reference for draft
rows with a replay start, and the two cases' draft windows begin at the
hit and at the kept row (CUDA).
